BspTree: bound the OUTDOORS sector counts, which size a resize and a read loop.

`BspTree::load`'s OUTDOORS (`0xC050`) branch reads three kinds of count off the
file and validates none of them:

	auto sector_count = c->read_uint();
	for (...) {
		sector.name = c->read_line(false);
		auto node_count = c->read_uint();
		auto polygon_count = c->read_uint();
		sector.node_indices.resize(node_count);
		sector.portal_polygon_indices.resize(polygon_count);

`proto::read_chunked` hands the callback the *whole* reader, not one bounded to
the chunk (`include/zenkit/Stream.hh`), so a corrupted count does not walk off
the end of anything that fails -- it simply reads on into the next chunk's
header, and past the end of the archive reads return nothing rather than
throwing. Neither the loop nor the `resize` can fail loudly on its own.

Found by fuzzing `test/fixtures/minimal.g2.zen` with `tools/fuzz-world.js`: seed
124 of the 200-seed run recorded in `docs/plans/level-editor.md` 16.11
delta-debugs down to **one byte**, file offset 1317, the low byte of
`sector_count`. It turns a world with no outdoor sectors into one declaring 121,
and the very first sector is already fatal: the name is the four zero bytes of
`portal_count`, so `node_count` is read across the following `0xC0FF` chunk
header as `0xFF000000` and `resize` commits 17 GB. Measured: one sector is
enough, `sector_count` of 1 hangs identically.

The fix bounds each count by the bytes actually left in the reader -- a sector
costs at least a name terminator and its two counts (9 bytes) and an index costs
4 -- which needs a `_bytes_remaining` helper, since `Read` exposes no size but
every implementation can seek to the end and back. It cannot change what a valid
world parses: a real world carries the data its counts declare, so the check is
only ever reached by a file whose BSP is already unreadable.

One more instance of the unvalidated-file-data class in 16.11, not the
class-level fix: `_parse_bsp_nodes` still recurses once per file-supplied flag
bit with no depth bound, the chunk-walking loops in `Mesh.cc` and the VOB readers
are untouched, and making `ReadMemory::seek` clamp or throw is still deferred.

Upstreamable as-is: a plain robustness bug, no API change, three guards.

